# Target C-II — direct-energy near-floor pentagon/rank normal form

**Date:** 2026-09-15<br>
**Repository base:** current `main` through the hostile-audited direct dominant/complement energy theorem<br>
**Status:** proof-only full-core Type-II strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged<br>
**Scope:** fully force-visible no-container/full-core Type-II branch<br>
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The direct dominant/complement outer-energy theorem now proves

\[
\boxed{\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.83236.}
\]

Its exact scalar relaxation has root `1.8323676277438298975...`.  More importantly for
closure, its contradiction margin is uniformly decreasing in the outer-detour variable.
This gives a stability theorem just above the scalar floor rather than only another
excluded endpoint.

For every full-core Type-II survivor with

\[
\boxed{t:={q-k\over D}\le1.8325,}
\]

the unused network slack is less than `0.00040920`.  The low-`t` face ledger identifies
that same slack with the available excess face degree.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{
{Q-f\over D}<0.00040920+o(1),
\qquad
{k\over D}<0.00163679+o(1),
}
\tag{NF.1}
\]

\[
\boxed{
{p_5\over D}>0.4962786-o(1),
}
\tag{NF.2}
\]

where `p_5` is the number of bounded pentagonal contact faces, and

\[
\boxed{
{\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}\over D}
>0.9917388-o(1).
}
\tag{NF.3}

An independent use of the audited complement-capacity theorem, extended over this
slightly wider range, still forces at least

\[
\boxed{0.4081989D-o(D)}
\tag{NF.4}

retained complement outer-to-outer stopped trains.  Every one is two-sidedly braced by
actual diameter edges by the audited Section-157 theorem.

Finally the `g=0.0050` all-train correlation and the current `t>=1.83236` floor give

\[
\boxed{
{\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R\over D}
\ge1.84236-o(1),
}
\tag{NF.5}

so every survivor has either at least `0.46059D-o(D)` singleton stopped trains or a
rhombic Mantel deficit exceeding `0.4916615D^2-o(D^2)`.

This is not C-II closure.  It replaces the low-`t` scalar endpoint by a concrete global
compatibility problem: an almost pure pentagon sea with almost full exceptional-face
closure rank must coexist with a linear family of diameter-braced outer-to-outer train
cuts and one of two large train-defect mechanisms.

---

## 1. Direct-energy margin and the network simplex

On a convergent full-core Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={b-P_F\over D}={d_{out}\over D}.
\]

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050,
\]

and

\[
R(t)={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0.
\]

The hostile-audited train theorem and Section 171 give

\[
\boxed{
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t).
}
\tag{NF.6}

Let

\[
\delta_{max}(t)={R(t)\over2}.
\]

The direct dominant/complement theorem defines

\[
G(t,\delta,\kappa)
=\min_K\left[
F(K-\delta)+J(\pi-K-N_*)
\right]-\delta,
\tag{NF.7}
\]

where

\[
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6),
\qquad
J(p)=2p-6\sin(p/3),
\]

and `N_*` is the complement-train population upper bound from that theorem.  Every
survivor satisfies

\[
G(t,\delta,\kappa)\le0.
\tag{NF.8}
\]

The hostile audit proves throughout the present interval that

\[
{\partial G\over\partial\kappa}>0
\tag{NF.9}
\]

and

\[
{\partial G\over\partial\delta}<-0.76.
\tag{NF.10}
\]

The companion verifier rechecks the stronger coarse upper bound
`-0.7713...` for (NF.10).

At `kappa=0`, `delta=delta_max(t)` let the minimized boundary margin be `G_0(t)`.  The
direct theorem proves `G_0` is strictly decreasing in `t`.  At the top of the present
band the verifier gives

\[
\boxed{
G_0(1.8325)
=-0.0003109891582858596\ldots .
}
\tag{NF.11}

For an actual survivor, (NF.9) implies

\[
G(t,\delta,0)\le G(t,\delta,\kappa)\le0.
\]

Integrating (NF.10) from `delta` to `delta_max(t)` gives

\[
G(t,\delta,0)
>
G_0(t)+0.76[\delta_{max}(t)-\delta].
\]

Since `G_0(t)>=G_0(1.8325)` for `t<=1.8325`, every survivor obeys

\[
\boxed{
\delta_{max}(t)-\delta
<0.000409196260902447\ldots .
}
\tag{NF.12}

This is the basic near-floor stability estimate.

The network simplex (NF.6) is equivalently

\[
\delta+{\kappa\over4}\le\delta_{max}(t),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\kappa<0.00163678504360979\ldots .
}
\tag{NF.13}

---

## 2. Exceptional degree is exactly paid by unused network slack

The low-`t` face ledger gives

\[
{Q\over D}=2t-\pi-\delta+o(1)
\tag{NF.14}
\]

and

\[
C={t\over2}+2{f\over D}-{\kappa\over2}\ge C_0,
\]

hence

\[
{f\over D}
\ge {C_0\over2}-{t\over4}+{\kappa\over4}-o(1).
\tag{NF.15}
\]

Subtracting (NF.15) from (NF.14),

\[
{Q-f\over D}
\le
{9t\over4}-\pi-{C_0\over2}-\delta-{\kappa\over4}+o(1).
\]

But

\[
{9t\over4}-\pi-{C_0\over2}
={R(t)\over2}
=\delta_{max}(t),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
{Q-f\over D}
\le
\delta_{max}(t)-\delta-{\kappa\over4}+o(1).
}
\tag{NF.16}

Thus the exceptional-degree slack is literally a subquantity of the unused network
slack.  Equations (NF.12) and (NF.16) prove

\[
\boxed{
{Q-f\over D}<0.000409196260902447+o(1).
}
\tag{NF.17}

Since

\[
Q-f=\sum_{F\text{ exceptional}}(\deg F-5),
\]

the number `f_{>=6}` of exceptional faces of degree at least six satisfies

\[
\boxed{
{f_{\ge6}\over D}<0.000409196260902447+o(1).
}
\tag{NF.18}

---

## 3. More than `0.4962786D` actual pentagons

For `t<=1.8325`, (NF.15) and `kappa>=0` give

\[
{f\over D}
\ge
{C_0\over2}-{1.8325\over4}-o(1)
=0.4966878068639573\ldots-o(1).
\tag{NF.19}

Every exceptional face is either a pentagon or has degree at least six, so

\[
p_5=f-f_{\ge6}.
\]

Using (NF.18)--(NF.19),

\[
\boxed{
{p_5\over D}
>0.4962786106030549\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{NF.20}

At most `0.0824%` of the guaranteed exceptional-face population can have degree at
least six.  Equivalently, more than `99.9176%` of that population is forced to be
pentagonal throughout the band.

---

## 4. The face-closure matrix has almost full row rank

Canonical notation has

\[
k=d_H+s_Z,
\qquad d_H,s_Z\ge0,
\]

so (NF.13) gives

\[
{s_Z\over D}
<0.00163678504360979\ldots .
\tag{NF.21}

The exact exceptional-face closure-corank identity is

\[
2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}
=\sigma_C
\le s_Z.
\tag{NF.22}

Combining (NF.19), (NF.21), and (NF.22),

\[
\boxed{
{\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}\over D}
>0.9917388286843049\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{NF.23}

This is exact row rank.  It does not supply a quantitative smallest-singular-value
bound for the almost-degenerate pentagon blocks.

---

## 5. A universal complement outer-to-outer train population still survives

The direct tradeoff optimizes the dominant/complement split, so its minimizing
`p` cannot itself be quoted as a universal complement-train lower bound.  Instead use
the independently audited complement-capacity theorem.

For actual complement retained pair mass it gives

\[
p_c
\ge
\pi-Q_\Phi(\delta)
-U-{t+\kappa\over2}+{4\Delta_2\over U},
\tag{NF.24}
\]

where `Q_Phi=Phi^{-1}` for the joint-route function `Phi`.  On the present band:

- `p_c` increases with `kappa`;
- `p_c` decreases with `delta`; and
- on `kappa=0`, `delta=delta_max(t)`, it decreases with `t`.

The only range extension needed beyond the older certificate is

\[
Q_\Phi(\delta)<1.81.
\]

The companion verifier checks

\[
\Phi'(1.81)<0.044,
\qquad
{1\over\Phi'(1.81)}>22.7,
\]

while the complement train-count term changes with `delta` at rate less than `4`.
Thus the derivative of (NF.24) with respect to `delta` is still below `-18.7`, proving
the same monotonicity with large slack.

At the worst point `t=1.8325`, `kappa=0`, `delta=delta_max`, the certificate gives

\[
\boxed{
p_c>0.4081989620519120\ldots .}
\tag{NF.25}

Retained weight on one train is at most one, hence

\[
\boxed{
L_{OO}^{comp}>0.4081989D-o(D).
}
\tag{NF.26}

Every train counted here is an actual outer-to-outer stopped train outside the dominant
atomic windows and is crossed by actual diameter edges with both transverse signs.

---

## 6. The correlated stopped-train split strengthens again

Let

\[
\rho_{rec}:=b+4f+Q-B_4=2S,
\qquad
\sigma_R:=B_4-4\sqrt{R_4}.
\]

The exact train identities give

\[
\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R=2T-4\sqrt{R_4}.
\tag{NF.27}

The hostile-audited `g=0.0050` theorem gives

\[
{T-q/2\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050-o(1),
\]

and rhombic saturation gives

\[
{\sqrt{R_4}\over D}\to\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q\over D}
\ge0.0100-o(1).
}
\tag{NF.28}

Current main gives `t>=1.83236-o(1)` and `q/D=t+kappa`, hence

\[
\boxed{
{\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R\over D}
\ge1.84236-o(1).
}
\tag{NF.29}

At least one of the following holds:

\[
\boxed{S\ge0.46059D-o(D)}
\tag{NF.30}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{\sigma_R\ge0.92118D-o(D).}
\tag{NF.31}
\]

In the second branch, the exact rationalization

\[
M_R={T_R^2\over4}-R_4
={\sigma_R\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}\right)
\]

yields

\[
\boxed{
{M_R\over D^2}
>0.4916615427-o(1).
}
\tag{NF.32}

The exact post-endpoint surplus identity also gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\mu_D
\ge
{2\pi+2\sqrt{\Delta_2}\over5}
+{1.84236\over10}
=1.8217981841815002\ldots .
}
\tag{NF.33}

No known universal upper bound on `mu_D` contradicts this lower bound.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

A survivor in the narrow but nonempty band

\[
1.83236\le t\le1.8325
\]

must realize all of the following simultaneously:

```text
>= 0.4962786 D actual pentagonal bounded contact faces
<  0.0004092 D higher-degree exceptional faces
<  0.0016368 D mixed-rigidity defect k
>= 0.9917388 D exceptional-face closure rank
>= 0.4081989 D complement outer-to-outer stopped trains
+ two-sided actual-diameter bracing on every such train
+ either >= 0.46059 D singleton trains
  or rhombic Mantel deficit > 0.4916615 D^2.
```

The remaining closure target is now highly specific.  One must prove that this
near-pentagonal, almost full-closure-rank contact complex cannot simultaneously host
the required globally braced train cuts and correlated singleton/rhombic defect.  A
local pentagon area or angle gap is not available because the flat triangular-lattice
pentagon regression remains valid.

No exact unit equilateral triangle or source-preserving strict sub-`D` competitor is
claimed here.

**Disposition:** proof-only near-floor structural strengthening. C-II and Level I
remain open.
